(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'Equilibrium','','easy','single','According to the Bronsted-Lowry theory, an acid is a:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Proton donor"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Proton acceptor"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Electron pair donor"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Electron pair acceptor"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'A Bronsted-Lowry acid is defined as a proton (H+) donor.','["Recall the three definitions of acids (Arrhenius, Bronsted, Lewis)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XI, Ch 7'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids','','easy','single','The reagent used in the Rosenmund reduction is:','[{"id": "a", "text": "LiAlH4"}, {"id": "b", "text": "NaBH4"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Zn-Hg/HCl"}, {"id": "d", "text": "H2/Pd-BaSO4"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Rosenmund reduction uses H2/Pd over BaSO4 (poisoned by sulfur or quinoline) to reduce acyl chlorides to aldehydes.','["Identify the reaction (Acyl chloride to Aldehyde).", "Match with Pd-BaSO4 catalyst."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XII, Ch 12'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'Redox Reactions','','easy','single','The oxidation state of Oxygen in OF2 is:','[{"id": "a", "text": "-2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "+2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "-1"}, {"id": "d", "text": "+1"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen, so F is -1 and O is +2.','["Apply electronegativity rules: F is always -1.", "x + 2(-1) = 0 -> x = +2."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XI, Ch 8'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties','','medium','single','Which of the following elements has the highest negative electron gain enthalpy?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Fluorine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Chlorine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oxygen"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sulfur"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Chlorine has the highest negative electron gain enthalpy because the 3p subshell is larger than 2p (Fluorine), resulting in less inter-electronic repulsion.','["Compare group 16 and 17: Halogens are more negative.", "Compare F and Cl.", "Cl has less repulsion due to larger size."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XI Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers','','medium','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The boiling point of p-nitrophenol is higher than that of o-nitrophenol.
Reason (R): o-nitrophenol shows intramolecular hydrogen bonding while p-nitrophenol shows intermolecular hydrogen bonding.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Intermolecular H-bonding in p-nitrophenol leads to association of molecules, increasing the boiling point compared to the chelation in o-nitrophenol.','["Identify H-bonding types: ortho is intra, para is inter.", "Intermolecular forces increase boiling point.", "Verify both statements and link."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XII Ch 7 (New) / Ch 11 (Old)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'Chemical Kinetics','','medium','single','For a reaction A + B -> Products, doubling the concentration of A doubles the rate, and doubling the concentration of B quadruples the rate. The overall order of the reaction is:','[{"id": "a", "text": "1"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "3"}, {"id": "d", "text": "4"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Rate = k[A]^1 [B]^2. The overall order is 1 + 2 = 3.','["Rate depends linearly on [A] (order 1).", "Rate depends on square of [B] (order 2).", "Sum of powers: 1 + 2 = 3."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XII Ch 3 (New) / Ch 4 (Old)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes / Amines','','medium','single','Which of the following compounds will not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Chlorobenzene"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Toluene"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aniline"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nitrobenzene (in presence of excess catalyst)"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Aniline forms a salt with AlCl3 (Lewis acid), which makes the nitrogen positively charged and strongly deactivates the ring.','["Identify the Lewis acid catalyst AlCl3.", "Check for base-acid interaction.", "Aniline lone pair reacts with AlCl3."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XII Ch 9 (New) / Ch 13 (Old)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'d and f Block Elements','','medium','single','The magnetic moment of a transition metal ion is 3.87 BM. The number of unpaired electrons present is:','[{"id": "a", "text": "2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "3"}, {"id": "c", "text": "4"}, {"id": "d", "text": "5"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Using the formula μ = sqrt(n(n+2)), where n=3, μ = sqrt(15) = 3.87.','["Apply μ = sqrt(n(n+2)).", "Check for n=3: sqrt(3*5) = sqrt(15).", "sqrt(15) is approx 3.87."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XII Ch 4 (New) / Ch 8 (Old)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','jee-main','jee-advanced','comedk','scholarship'],'Chemistry','Class 11-12',2026,'p-Block Elements (Group 14)','','medium','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Graphite is a good conductor of electricity whereas diamond is an insulator.
Reason (R): In graphite, every carbon atom is sp2 hybridized and has one free electron per atom.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The delocalized pi-electrons in graphite''s sp2 hybridized layers allow for electrical conductivity, which diamond lacks due to sp3 hybridization.','["Examine hybridization: Diamond sp3, Graphite sp2.", "Free electrons in graphite provide conductivity.", "Confirm both statements are correct."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Chemistry XI Ch 11 (Old) / P-block fundamentals XII'),
