(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Anatomy of Flowering Plants','','medium','single','Which of the following describes the nature of the vascular bundles in a monocot stem?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Arranged in a ring, open, and endarch."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Scattered, open, and exarch."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arranged in a ring, closed, and exarch."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Scattered, closed, and endarch."}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Monocot stems have scattered vascular bundles that are conjoint, closed (no cambium), and endarch.','["Recall monocot stem bundle distribution (scattered).", "Check for cambium (closed).", "Check protoxylem position (endarch)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 6'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','medium','single','Which pigment acts as the reaction center in Photosystem II?','[{"id": "a", "text": "P700"}, {"id": "b", "text": "P680"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Chlorophyll b"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Xanthophyll"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'PS II has an absorption peak at 680 nm, hence its reaction center is P680.','["Distinguish PS I and PS II reaction centers.", "Associate 680 nm with PS II."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','hard','single','In the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis, if a chloroplast is supplied with water containing heavy isotope of oxygen (18O) and CO2 containing 17O, which of the following is true regarding the products?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Oxygen gas evolved will contain 17O."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The sugar formed will contain 18O."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oxygen gas evolved will contain 18O."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both oxygen gas and sugar will contain 18O."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Oxygen evolved during photosynthesis comes exclusively from the photolysis of water, not from carbon dioxide.','["Identify the source of O2 evolution (H2O).", "Identify the source of Oxygen in Glucose (CO2).", "Apply isotopic labels: 18O from H2O goes to O2 gas.", "17O from CO2 goes to Glucose and metabolic water."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): In a C4 plant like Maize, the C4 pathway consumes more ATP than the C3 pathway to fix one molecule of CO2.
Reason (R): The regeneration of Phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) from Pyruvate in the mesophyll cells requires the conversion of ATP to AMP.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'C4 plants use 2 extra ATP (equivalent) per CO2 to regenerate PEP, totaling 5 ATP per CO2 compared to 3 ATP in C3.','["Recall ATP cost: C3 = 3 ATP/CO2; C4 = 5 ATP/CO2.", "Analyze regeneration step: Pyruvate + ATP + Pi -> PEP + AMP + PPi.", "AMP production is energetically equivalent to 2 ATP hydrolyses."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Plant Kingdom','','hard','single','A specific gymnosperm species has a diploid chromosome number of 24 (2n = 24). What would be the chromosome number in its endosperm and its microspore mother cell respectively?','[{"id": "a", "text": "36 and 24"}, {"id": "b", "text": "12 and 24"}, {"id": "c", "text": "24 and 12"}, {"id": "d", "text": "36 and 12"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'In gymnosperms, endosperm is a pre-fertilization haploid tissue (n), while microspore mother cells are diploid (2n).','["Identify Gymnosperm endosperm ploidy = n.", "Identify MMC ploidy = 2n.", "Calculate: n = 24/2 = 12.", "MMC = 2n = 24."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','hard','single','How many molecules of reduced NADP and ATP are required to produce one molecule of sucrose through the Calvin cycle?','[{"id": "a", "text": "18 ATP and 12 NADPH"}, {"id": "b", "text": "30 ATP and 12 NADPH"}, {"id": "c", "text": "30 ATP and 20 NADPH"}, {"id": "d", "text": "36 ATP and 24 NADPH"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Sucrose is a disaccharide (12C). One CO2 requires 3 ATP and 2 NADPH. Total: 12 x 3 = 36 ATP and 12 x 2 = 24 NADPH.','["Sucrose = Glucose + Fructose (12 carbons).", "Calvin cycle cost per C = 3 ATP, 2 NADPH.", "Multiply by 12: 12 * 3 = 36 ATP.", "Multiply by 12: 12 * 2 = 24 NADPH."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Respiration in Plants','','hard','single','In an experiment, a researcher inhibits the enzyme Aconitase in the TCA cycle. Which of the following intermediates will accumulate in the mitochondrial matrix?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Oxaloacetic acid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acetyl CoA"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Citric acid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "alpha-Ketoglutaric acid"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Aconitase catalyzes the conversion of Citrate to Isocitrate. Inhibition causes the accumulation of the substrate, Citrate.','["Locate Aconitase in the TCA cycle.", "Substrate = Citrate; Product = Isocitrate.", "Enzyme block leads to substrate build-up."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 14'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Respiration in Plants','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): During seed germination, the Respiratory Quotient (RQ) for castor seeds is less than one.
Reason (R): Castor seeds contain high amounts of fats, which require more oxygen for complete oxidation compared to the amount of CO2 released.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Fats have an RQ of approximately 0.7 because they are oxygen-poor and require more external O2 for oxidation.','["Castor is an oil-seed (fats).", "RQ of fats = 0.7 (less than 1).", "Reason explains the stoichiometry of fat oxidation."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 14'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Respiration in Plants','','hard','single','Which of the following represents the correct sequence of electron carriers in the mitochondrial ETS between Complex III and Complex IV?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Ubiquinone -> Cyt b -> Cyt c1"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cyt c1 -> Cyt c -> Cyt a-a3"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cyt b -> Cyt c -> Cyt c1"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cyt a -> Cyt a3 -> Cyt c"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Cytochrome c is a small, mobile protein located on the outer surface of the inner mitochondrial membrane that transfers electrons from Complex III to IV.','["Complex III contains Cyt b and Cyt c1.", "Complex IV contains Cyt a and Cyt a3.", "Cytochrome c acts as a mobile carrier between III and IV."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 14'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Plant Growth and Development (Water Relations context)','','hard','single','A plant cell is placed in a solution with a water potential of -10 bars. The cell has an osmotic potential (solute potential) of -15 bars and a turgor pressure (pressure potential) of 3 bars. In which direction will water move?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Water will exit the cell because the cell has higher potential."}, {"id": "b", "text": "No net movement as the system is in equilibrium."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Water will enter the cell because the external solution has a higher potential."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The cell will undergo plasmolysis."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Cell Psi = Psi_s + Psi_p = -15 + 3 = -12 bars. Water moves from higher potential (-10) to lower potential (-12), so water enters the cell.','["Calculate Cell Psi_w = -15 + 3 = -12.", "Identify External Psi_w = -10.", "Compare: -10 > -12.", "Water flows from higher to lower: External to Cell."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 15 (concept from Ch 11 old)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Principles of Inheritance and Variation','','hard','single','Consider a population of 100 plants produced from a cross between two heterozygous tall plants (Tt). What is the probability that a randomly selected plant from the F1 generation is both tall and homozygous?','[{"id": "a", "text": "0.25"}, {"id": "b", "text": "0.50"}, {"id": "c", "text": "0.75"}, {"id": "d", "text": "0.33"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'In a Tt x Tt cross, genotypes are 1TT:2Tt:1tt. Tall plants are TT and Tt. Homozygous tall is TT, which is 1/4 of the total.','["Determine Punnett square: TT, Tt, Tt, tt.", "Identify ''Tall and Homozygous'' = TT.", "TT frequency = 1 out of 4.", "1/4 = 0.25."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 4 (old 5)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Some plants, like Viola (common pansy), produce two types of flowers: chasmogamous and cleistogamous.
Reason (R): Cleistogamous flowers ensure seed set even in the absence of pollinators by preventing cross-pollination.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Both statements are true. However, R is a characteristic/advantage of cleistogamy, but doesn''t explain *why* the plant produces two *types* (which is an evolutionary strategy for bet-hedging).','["Identify Viola as a producer of both flower types.", "Define Cleistogamy: closed flowers, guaranteed autogamy.", "Evaluate if R explains the *existence* of both (R only describes one)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 1 (old 2)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance','','hard','single','If a DNA molecule has 20% Guanine, what will be the percentage of Adenine and the total percentage of purines respectively?','[{"id": "a", "text": "20% and 40%"}, {"id": "b", "text": "30% and 40%"}, {"id": "c", "text": "20% and 50%"}, {"id": "d", "text": "30% and 50%"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'G = 20%, so C = 20%. G+C = 40%. A+T = 60%, so A = 30%. Purines (A+G) = 30 + 20 = 50%. Total purines always = 50% in dsDNA.','["Chargaff''s rule: A=T, G=C.", "If G=20, then C=20. G+C=40.", "A+T = 100-40 = 60. A=30.", "Purines = A+G = 30+20 = 50."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 5 (old 6)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants','','hard','single','A typical dicot embryo at the heart-shaped stage consists of which of the following?','[{"id": "a", "text": "A globular mass of undifferentiated cells."}, {"id": "b", "text": "A single shield-shaped cotyledon called scutellum."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Two cotyledons and a central embryonal axis."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Only a suspensor and a radicle."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'A heart-shaped embryo shows two distinct lobes which will become the two cotyledons, and the developing radicle and plumule area.','["Recall embryogenesis stages: Proembryo -> Globular -> Heart-shaped -> Mature.", "Heart shape is defined by the emergence of the two cotyledonary primordia."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 1 (old 2)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance','','hard','single','In the DNA fingerprinting technique, what is the specific role of ''VNTRs''?','[{"id": "a", "text": "They act as primers for PCR amplification."}, {"id": "b", "text": "They serve as probes that show high polymorphism in the population."}, {"id": "c", "text": "They are the enzymes used to cut the DNA at specific sites."}, {"id": "d", "text": "They code for specific proteins involved in skin pigmentation."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'VNTRs (Variable Number of Tandem Repeats) belong to a class of satellite DNA referred to as mini-satellites, used as probes for their high degree of polymorphism.','["Define VNTR: Variable Number of Tandem Repeats.", "Function in DNA profiling: High variability between individuals.", "Usage: Radiolabelled VNTRs act as probes in Southern hybridization."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 5 (old 6)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Cell Cycle and Cell Division','','hard','single','During which phase of the cell cycle in onion root tip cells do the chromosomes align at the equatorial plate and for which reason?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Metaphase; due to balanced tension from spindle fibers attached to kinetochores."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Anaphase; due to the shortening of microtubules."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prophase; due to condensation of chromatin into chromosomes."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Telophase; due to the reformation of the nuclear envelope."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Metaphase is characterized by alignment at the equator, which is facilitated by the attachment of spindle fibers to the kinetochores of sister chromatids.','["Identify Metaphase: Chromosomes at equator.", "Mechanism: Kinetochore attachment and spindle tension."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 10'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Principles of Inheritance and Variation','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): In a monohybrid cross, the F2 phenotypic ratio 3:1 is a result of the random fusion of gametes.
Reason (R): According to the Law of Segregation, the alleles do not show any blending and both characters are recovered as such in the F2 generation.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Both are true, but the Law of Segregation explains why gametes are pure and why the recessive trait reappears, but the specific 3:1 ratio is a statistical outcome of the random fusion of those segregated gametes.','["A: Phenotypic ratio 3:1 is due to random fusion of (3:1) combination of alleles.", "R: Segregation ensures gametes carry one allele.", "Check link: Segregation is the mechanism; random fusion is the application."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 4 (old 5)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants','','hard','single','How many meiotic divisions are required to produce 200 seeds in a typical angiosperm plant like Pea?','[{"id": "a", "text": "200"}, {"id": "b", "text": "400"}, {"id": "c", "text": "250"}, {"id": "d", "text": "500"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'1 seed = 1 male gametophyte + 1 female gametophyte. To get 200 pollen grains: 200/4 = 50 meiosis. To get 200 eggs: 200/1 = 200 meiosis. Total = 250.','["1 seed needs 1 pollen + 1 egg.", "Meiosis for 200 pollen: 200 / 4 = 50.", "Meiosis for 200 eggs: 200 / 1 = 200 (3 degenerate).", "Total meiosis = 50 + 200 = 250."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 1 (old 2)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','hard','single','Which of the following statements regarding photorespiration is INCORRECT?','[{"id": "a", "text": "It involves the interaction of chloroplasts, peroxisomes, and mitochondria."}, {"id": "b", "text": "RuBisCO binds with O2 instead of CO2 at high temperatures."}, {"id": "c", "text": "It results in the loss of fixed carbon as CO2."}, {"id": "d", "text": "It produces high-energy phosphates like ATP and NADPH."}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Photorespiration involves the chloroplast, peroxisome, and mitochondrion, but it does NOT result in the synthesis of ATP or NADPH; it actually consumes ATP.','["Review C2 cycle (Photorespiration).", "Organs involved: CP, P, M (Correct).", "Mechanism: RuBisCO oxygenase activity (Correct).", "Outcome: Wasteful, no ATP/NADPH made (Incorrect statement found)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance','','hard','single','In a lac operon, if a mutation occurs in the ''i'' gene such that it cannot bind to the inducer (lactose), what will be the state of the operon in the presence of lactose?','[{"id": "a", "text": "The operon will be constitutively active (always ON)."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The operon will be transcribed only when glucose is absent."}, {"id": "c", "text": "The operon will be permanently repressed (always OFF)."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transcription will occur normally but translation will fail."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'If the repressor (i gene product) cannot bind to the inducer, it remains bound to the operator, keeping the operon permanently ''off'' (constitutively repressed).','["Normal function: Inducer binds Repressor -> Repressor leaves Operator -> ON.", "Mutant: Inducer cannot bind Repressor -> Repressor stays on Operator -> OFF."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 5 (old 6)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The primary CO2 acceptor in C3 plants is a 5-carbon ketose sugar.
Reason (R): The first stable product of the Calvin cycle is a 3-carbon organic acid.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Both are true. The acceptor is Ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate (RuBP - 5C) and the first product is 3-PGA (3C). However, R is a consequence, not a reason for A''s identity.','["Identify C3 acceptor: RuBP (5C sugar). (True)", "Identify C3 first product: 3-PGA (3C acid). (True)", "Does the product explain the acceptor? No, they are just features of the cycle."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Plant Growth and Development','','hard','single','Which plant hormone is responsible for the ''Triple Response'' in seedlings and promotes the senescence and abscission of plant organs?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Ethylene"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Abscisic Acid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Gibberellic Acid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cytokinin"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Ethylene induces triple response (shortening, thickening, and horizontal growth of axis) and is a promoter of aging (senescence).','["Link Triple Response to Ethylene.", "Link Abscission/Senescence to Ethylene (and ABA).", "Ethylene is the primary hormone for the specific ''triple response''."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 15'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance','','hard','single','A gene has 6000 base pairs. How many nucleosomes will be present in the chromatin of this gene segment?','[{"id": "a", "text": "60"}, {"id": "b", "text": "30"}, {"id": "c", "text": "200"}, {"id": "d", "text": "12000"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'One nucleosome typically contains 200 bp of DNA. 6000 / 200 = 30 nucleosomes.','["Standard nucleosome DNA length = 200 bp.", "Total length = 6000 bp.", "Divide: 6000 / 200 = 30."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 5 (old 6)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','hard','single','During cyclic photophosphorylation, which of the following is NOT produced?','[{"id": "a", "text": "ATP"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Light-driven electron flow"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Proton gradient"}, {"id": "d", "text": "NADPH and O2 evolution"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Cyclic photophosphorylation only involves PS I and results in ATP synthesis. O2 evolution and NADPH synthesis only occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation.','["Cyclic flow involves PS I only.", "Electrons return to the reaction center.", "No photolysis of water (so no O2).", "No reduction of NADP+ (so no NADPH)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Anatomy of Flowering Plants / Plant Kingdom','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Vessels are a characteristic feature of angiosperms and are absent in all gymnosperms.
Reason (R): The presence of vessels is an evolutionary advancement for more efficient water transport.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'While vessels are typical of angiosperms, they are NOT absent in *all* gymnosperms; the Order Gnetales (e.g., Gnetum, Ephedra) possesses vessels. R is true.','["Check the ''all'' condition in A. Gnetophytes have vessels. A is false.", "Check R: Vessels have wider lumens and perforated plates, improving efficiency. R is true."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 3 & 6'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Plant Growth and Development (Water Relations)','','hard','single','In a plant, the process of guttation occurs through specialized structures called ______ when ______.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Stomata; transpiration rate is high."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Lenticels; atmospheric humidity is low."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hydathodes; root pressure is high and transpiration is low."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Trichomes; soil is dry."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Guttation occurs through hydathodes due to high root pressure and low transpiration (usually at night or early morning).','["Define Guttation: Exudation of liquid water.", "Identify Structure: Hydathodes.", "Identify Conditions: High root pressure, low evaporation."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 15 (concept from Ch 11 old)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants','','hard','single','What is the ploidy level of the following tissues respectively: Perisperm, Aleurone layer, Scutellum?','[{"id": "a", "text": "2n, 2n, 3n"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2n, 3n, 2n"}, {"id": "c", "text": "3n, 3n, 2n"}, {"id": "d", "text": "n, 3n, 2n"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Perisperm is persistent nucellus (2n). Aleurone layer is part of endosperm (3n). Scutellum is the cotyledon of embryo (2n).','["Perisperm = Nucellus = 2n.", "Aleurone = Endosperm = 3n.", "Scutellum = Embryo = 2n."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 1 (old 2)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Principles of Inheritance and Variation','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): In Mirabilis jalapa, a cross between a red-flowered plant and a white-flowered plant produces pink flowers.
Reason (R): This is due to the phenomenon of Codominance where both alleles express themselves equally in the F1 hybrid.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'A is true, but the phenomenon is Incomplete Dominance (blending effect), not Codominance (where both traits appear, like AB blood group).','["Identify Mirabilis jalapa: 4 o''clock plant.", "Check Phenotype: Red x White = Pink. (True).", "Check mechanism: Incomplete dominance. (R says Codominance, so R is false)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 4 (old 5)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance','','hard','single','In DNA replication, the leading strand is synthesized ______ while the lagging strand is synthesized ______ due to the ______ activity of DNA polymerase.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Continuously; discontinuously; 5'' -> 3''"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Discontinuously; continuously; 3'' -> 5''"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Continuously; discontinuously; 3'' -> 5''"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discontinuously; continuously; 5'' -> 3''"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'DNA polymerase only works in the 5'' to 3'' direction. This necessitates continuous synthesis on the leading strand and discontinuous (Okazaki fragments) on the lagging strand.','["Leading strand = continuous.", "Lagging strand = discontinuous (Okazaki).", "Reason: Polymerization direction is always 5'' to 3''."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 5 (old 6)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Plant Growth and Development','','hard','single','Which of the following describes the correct condition for a ''Long Day Plant'' to flower?','[{"id": "a", "text": "The night length must exceed a critical duration."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The day length must be exactly 12 hours."}, {"id": "c", "text": "They only flower in the presence of high Abscisic acid."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The duration of light exceeds the critical photoperiod."}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Long Day Plants flower when the duration of light exceeds a critical photoperiod, or more accurately, when the dark period is shorter than a critical duration.','["Define LDP: Photoperiod > Critical period.", "Check options against definition."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 15'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','hard','single','Calculate the number of turns of the Calvin cycle and the number of CO2 molecules required to produce 3 molecules of Glucose.','[{"id": "a", "text": "6 turns and 6 CO2 molecules"}, {"id": "b", "text": "18 turns and 18 CO2 molecules"}, {"id": "c", "text": "12 turns and 12 CO2 molecules"}, {"id": "d", "text": "18 turns and 6 CO2 molecules"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'1 Glucose requires 6 turns and 6 CO2. 3 Glucose = 18 turns and 18 CO2.','["1 Glucose = 6 Carbon atoms.", "1 Calvin turn fixes 1 Carbon (1 CO2).", "3 Glucose = 18 Carbons.", "Need 18 turns and 18 CO2."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Cell: The Unit of Life / Respiration in Plants','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The inner membrane of the mitochondrion is folded into numerous cristae.
