(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Excretory Products and Their Elimination','','easy','single','The presence of which of the following in urine is an indicator of Diabetes Mellitus?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Glucose and Ketone bodies"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Urea and Uric acid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Proteins and Blood"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Water and Salt only"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Presence of Glucose (Glycosuria) and Ketone bodies (Ketonuria) in urine are indicative of Diabetes Mellitus.','["Recall clinical diagnosis via urine analysis.", "Identify markers for Diabetes."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 19 (Excretory Products and Their Elimination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants','','expert','single','In a typical angiosperm, if the megaspore mother cell undergoes meiosis to form four megaspores, but only one remains functional, what is the total number of mitotic divisions required to form a mature 7-celled, 8-nucleate embryo sac from that single functional megaspore?','[{"id": "a", "text": "1"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "3"}, {"id": "d", "text": "4"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'The functional megaspore undergoes three sequential rounds of free nuclear mitosis (1 to 2, 2 to 4, 4 to 8 nuclei) before cellularization.','["Functional megaspore is (n).", "1st mitosis = 2 nuclei.", "2nd mitosis = 4 nuclei.", "3rd mitosis = 8 nuclei."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 2 (New Ch 1)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): In C4 plants, the bundle sheath cells are characterized by a large number of chloroplasts, thick walls impervious to gaseous exchange and no intercellular spaces.
Reason (R): The presence of PEPcase in bundle sheath cells ensures high CO2 concentration around RuBisCO.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Assertion is correct, but the Reason is false because PEPcase is found in mesophyll cells, not bundle sheath cells (which contain RuBisCO).','["Anatomy of C4 plants (Kranz anatomy) matches A.", "PEPcase is the primary CO2 fixer in mesophyll.", "RuBisCO is in bundle sheath."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13 (New Ch 11)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Transport in Plants (Historical/Conceptual)','','expert','single','Consider the following sequence of water potential (Ψw) in a soil-plant-air continuum: Soil = -0.1 MPa, Root Xylem = -0.5 MPa, Leaf Mesophyll = -1.5 MPa, Atmosphere = -100 MPa. Which of the following statements regarding the movement of water is correct?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Water will move from Leaf to Root xylem."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Water will move spontaneously from Soil to Atmosphere through the plant."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Water movement will stop at the Root Xylem due to negative pressure."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The gradient is incorrect as soil must have the lowest potential."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Water always moves from a region of higher water potential (less negative) to lower water potential (more negative).','["Water potential (Ψw) is highest in soil (-0.1).", "Ψw is lowest in atmosphere (-100).", "Movement: -0.1 -> -0.5 -> -1.5 -> -100."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 11 (Removed in 2024 but concept applies to Plant Phys)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','expert','single','How many net ATP and NADPH molecules are required for the net synthesis of one molecule of sucrose in a C3 plant?','[{"id": "a", "text": "36 ATP, 24 NADPH2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "30 ATP, 12 NADPH2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "18 ATP, 12 NADPH2"}, {"id": "d", "text": "60 ATP, 30 NADPH2"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'One glucose needs 18 ATP/12 NADPH. Sucrose (C12H22O11) consists of two hexoses, so 18x2=36 ATP and 12x2=24 NADPH.','["Calvin cycle: 1 CO2 = 3 ATP, 2 NADPH.", "1 Glucose (6C) = 18 ATP, 12 NADPH.", "1 Sucrose (12C) = 36 ATP, 24 NADPH."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13 (New Ch 11)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants / Genetics','','expert','single','In a hexaploid (6n) wheat plant, what would be the ploidy of the endosperm and the microspore mother cell respectively?','[{"id": "a", "text": "3n, 6n"}, {"id": "b", "text": "6n, 3n"}, {"id": "c", "text": "9n, 3n"}, {"id": "d", "text": "9n, 6n"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'If the plant is 6n, gametes are 3n. Endosperm (fusion of 2 polar nuclei + 1 male gamete) = 3n + 3n + 3n = 9n. MMC is somatic tissue = 6n.','["Plant (sporophyte) = 6n.", "Microspore Mother Cell = 6n.", "Gametophyte (n) = 3n.", "Endosperm = 2 polar nuclei (3n each) + male gamete (3n) = 9n."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 2 (New Ch 1)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The primary CO2 acceptor in the C3 cycle is a 5-carbon ketose sugar.
Reason (R): The first stable product of the C3 cycle is a 3-carbon organic acid.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Both A (RuBP is a 5-C ketose) and R (3-PGA) are correct facts, but R does not explain why RuBP is the acceptor.','["RuBP = Ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate (5C ketose).", "1st stable product = 3-phosphoglyceric acid (3C)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13 (New Ch 11)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','expert','single','Identify the correct sequence of electron flow in the Z-scheme of light reaction.','[{"id": "a", "text": "PS I -> Pheophytin -> PS II -> NADP+"}, {"id": "b", "text": "PS II -> Plastoquinone -> Cytochrome b6f -> Plastocyanin -> PS I -> Ferredoxin -> NADP+"}, {"id": "c", "text": "PS II -> Plastocyanin -> Cytochrome b6f -> PS I -> NADP+"}, {"id": "d", "text": "PS I -> Plastocyanin -> PS II -> NADP+"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Electrons move from PS II to Cytochrome b6f complex, then to PS I, and finally to NADP+.','["PS II (P680) excites first.", "PQ -> Cyt b6f -> PC are intermediates.", "PS I (P700) receives from PC and passes to Fd."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13 (New Ch 11)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Plant Growth and Development','','expert','single','A plant hormone is used to speed up the malting process in the brewing industry and increase the length of the axis in grapes. This hormone also:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Promotes nutrient mobilization and delays senescence. (Cytokinin)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Promotes female flowers in cucumbers. (Ethylene)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Promotes bolting in beet and cabbage. (GA)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Induces parthenocarpy in tomatoes. (Auxin - NCERT specific)"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'The hormone is Gibberellic acid (GA), which also causes bolting in rosette plants like beet/cabbage.','["Malting/Grape length = GA.", "GA causes bolting in beet, cabbage."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 15 (New Ch 13)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Anatomy of Flowering Plants','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Conjoint, collateral, and open vascular bundles are found in the stems of dicotyledonous plants.
Reason (R): Open vascular bundles possess a layer of cambium between xylem and phloem, enabling secondary growth.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The presence of cambium (Open) is exactly why they are called open and capable of secondary growth.','["Dicot stem = Conjoint, Collateral, Open.", "Open = Cambium present."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 6 (New Ch 5)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Plant Kingdom','','expert','single','In the life cycle of Ectocarpus and Fucus respectively, the patterns of life cycle are:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Haplontic, Diplontic"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Haplo-diplontic, Haplontic"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Diplontic, Haplo-diplontic"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Haplo-diplontic, Diplontic"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Ectocarpus shows Haplo-diplontic, while Fucus (an alga exception) is Diplontic.','["Ectocarpus, Polysiphonia, Kelps = Haplo-diplontic.", "Fucus = Diplontic."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Organisms and Populations','','expert','single','If a population of a plant species follows the equation dN/dt = rN[(K-N)/K], and the carrying capacity (K) is 500 while the current population (N) is 500, what is the growth rate?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Maximum"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Zero"}, {"id": "c", "text": "rN"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infinite"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'When N = K, the term (K-N)/K becomes zero, making dN/dt = 0. The population is at equilibrium.','["Logistic growth formula.", "Substitute N = 500, K = 500.", "(500-500)/500 = 0."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 13 (New Ch 11)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Morphology of Flowering Plants','','expert','single','Which of the following represents the correct floral formula for a plant belonging to the family Fabaceae?','[{"id": "a", "text": "% ♀ ⚥ K(5) C1+2+(2) A(9)+1 G1 (underline G)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "⊕ ⚥ K(5) C(5) A5 G(2) (underline G)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "⊕ ⚥ P(3+3) A3+3 G(3) (underline G)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "% ⚥ K(5) C5 A10 G1 (overbar G)"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Fabaceae is zygomorphic (%), bisexual, 5 sepals (gamosepalous), 1+2+(2) petals (papilionaceous), 9+1 stamens (diadelphous), and superior ovary.','["Fabaceae = Pea family.", "Zygomorphic and diadelphous.", "Check for G1 (superior)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 5'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','expert','single','During photorespiration, the decarboxylation step (release of CO2) occurs in which organelle?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Chloroplast"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Peroxisome"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Mitochondria"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Glyoxysome"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'CO2 is released during the conversion of two glycine (2C) molecules to one serine (3C) in the mitochondria.','["Photorespiration path: Chloroplast -> Peroxisome -> Mitochondria.", "Glycine is formed in peroxisome.", "Glycine -> Serine + CO2 + NH3 occurs in mitochondria."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13 (New Ch 11)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Biomolecules / Respiration','','expert','single','In the competitive inhibition of succinate dehydrogenase by malonate, which of the following is true?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Vmax increases, Km decreases"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vmax decreases, Km constant"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Vmax constant, Km decreases"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vmax constant, Km increases"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'In competitive inhibition, Km increases (more substrate needed to reach half Vmax) but Vmax remains unchanged.','["Competitive inhibitor competes for active site.", "High substrate concentration overcomes inhibition (Vmax same).", "Affinity appears lower (Km increases)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 9 (Biomolecules)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Cell: The Unit of Life','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The inner membrane of mitochondria is folded into cristae.
Reason (R): Cristae increase the surface area for ATP synthesis and electron transport.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The folding is specifically to increase the surface area available for the ETS and ATP synthase activity.','["Mitochondrial structure.", "Cristae = infoldings.", "Function = Surface area for enzymes."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 8'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Respiration in Plants','','expert','single','How many turns of the Krebs cycle are required for the complete oxidation of one molecule of sucrose?','[{"id": "a", "text": "1"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "3"}, {"id": "d", "text": "4"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Sucrose = 1 Glucose + 1 Fructose (Total 2 hexoses). Each hexose = 2 Pyruvates = 2 turns of Krebs cycle. Total = 4 turns.','["1 Sucrose = 2 Hexoses (Glucose/Fructose).", "1 Hexose -> 2 Pyruvates.", "1 Pyruvate -> 1 TCA cycle turn.", "2 hexoses = 4 turns."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 14 (New Ch 12)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants','','expert','single','In a monoecious plant like Maize, which of the following is prevented by its flowering habit?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both Autogamy and Geitonogamy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Autogamy only"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Geitonogamy only"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neither Autogamy nor Geitonogamy"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'In monoecious plants (male/female flowers on same plant), autogamy (same flower) is prevented, but geitonogamy (different flower, same plant) can occur.','["Monoecious = Unisexual flowers on same plant.", "Autogamy needs bisexual flower.", "Geitonogamy needs male/female on same plant."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 2 (New Ch 1)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Transport in Plants (Historical/Conceptual)','','expert','single','Which of the following statements about the ''Mass Flow Hypothesis'' is INCORRECT?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Phloem loading is an active process."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Water moves from xylem to phloem at the source due to water potential gradient."}, {"id": "c", "text": "At the sink, sugar is actively unloaded."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Loading of sugar at the source creates a hypotonic condition in the sieve tube."}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Sugar is loaded into the sieve tube by active transport, creating a hypertonic condition (not hypotonic).','["Sugar loading = Active.", "Increases solute concentration = Hypertonic.", "Water enters from xylem."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 11 (Removed/Conceptual)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Anatomy of Flowering Plants','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): In the dicot root, the vascular cambium is completely secondary in origin.
Reason (R): It originates from tissue located just below the phloem bundles and a portion of pericycle tissue above the protoxylem.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Unlike stems, root cambium is not pre-existing; it forms later from these specific locations.','["Root secondary growth.", "Cambium origin = below phloem + pericycle.", "This makes it secondary."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 6 (New Ch 5)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Transport in Plants / Plant Phys','','expert','single','Match the following:
(a) Guttation  (i) Pressure in Xylem
(b) Transpiration (ii) Root Pressure
(c) Cohesion  (iii) Pulling force
(d) Adhesion  (iv) Mutual attraction between water molecules
(e) Surface Tension (v) Attraction to polar surfaces','[{"id": "a", "text": "a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv, e-v"}, {"id": "b", "text": "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-v, e-i (e is not i)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-v, e-(attraction in liquid phase)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "a-iii, b-ii, c-v, d-i, e-iv"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Guttation is due to root pressure (ii). Cohesion is mutual attraction (iv). Adhesion is to polar surfaces (v).','["Guttation = Root Pressure.", "Transpiration = Pull.", "Cohesion = Water-Water.", "Adhesion = Water-Wall."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 11 / Ch 14'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','expert','single','In the C4 pathway, the first CO2 fixation occurs in ____ and the second CO2 fixation occurs in ____ respectively.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Mesophyll, Mesophyll"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bundle sheath, Mesophyll"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bundle sheath, Bundle sheath"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mesophyll, Bundle sheath"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Primary fixation by PEPcase in mesophyll, secondary fixation by RuBisCO in bundle sheath.','["Mesophyll (PEPcase).", "Malic acid moves to Bundle Sheath.", "Decarboxylation releases CO2 for RuBisCO."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13 (New Ch 11)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Exine of pollen grain is made of sporopollenin which is one of the most resistant organic materials known.
Reason (R): No enzyme that degrades sporopollenin is so far known.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The chemical resistance of sporopollenin is precisely why it remains intact in fossils and is not degraded by enzymes.','["Pollen wall = Exine.", "Composition = Sporopollenin.", "Resistance properties."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 2 (New Ch 1)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Transport in Plants / Plant Phys','','expert','single','A tree girdled up to the depth of the phloem will eventually die because:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Water cannot reach the leaves."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sugars cannot reach the roots."}, {"id": "c", "text": "The bark provides mechanical support."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Respiration in the stem stops."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Girdling (ringing) removes phloem, preventing downward movement of sugars to the roots, causing them to starve first.','["Girdling removes phloem.", "Phloem transports food (sugars).", "Roots are sinks for food from leaves."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 11 (Removed but foundational)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Anatomy of Flowering Plants','','expert','single','During the secondary growth of a dicot stem, the activity of the vascular cambium produces:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Equal amounts of secondary xylem and secondary phloem."}, {"id": "b", "text": "More secondary phloem than secondary xylem."}, {"id": "c", "text": "More secondary xylem than secondary phloem."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Primary xylem and primary phloem only."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Vascular cambium is generally more active on the inner side, producing more secondary xylem than secondary phloem.','["Vascular cambium (meristematic).", "Inner production = Sec. Xylem.", "Outer production = Sec. Phloem.", "Rate of xylem production is higher."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 6 (New Ch 5)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Respiration in Plants','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): All the enzymes of the TCA cycle are located in the mitochondrial matrix.
Reason (R): TCA cycle is the second stage of aerobic respiration.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Assertion is false because Succinate Dehydrogenase is located on the inner mitochondrial membrane, not the matrix. Reason is true.','["TCA enzymes are mostly in matrix.", "Exception: Succinate Dehydrogenase (Inner membrane)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 14 (New Ch 12) / Ch 9'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Photosynthesis in Higher Plants','','expert','single','In the light reaction of photosynthesis, the movement of protons to create a gradient across the thylakoid membrane is driven by:
I. Splitting of water on the inner side of the membrane.
II. Primary electron acceptor passing electrons to an H-carrier.
III. NADP reductase located on the stroma side.
Which of these are correct?','[{"id": "a", "text": "I and II only"}, {"id": "b", "text": "II and III only"}, {"id": "c", "text": "I and III only"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I, II and III"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'All three mechanisms contribute: Water splitting (internal protons), H-carrier (pumping protons in), and NADP+ reduction (removing stroma protons).','["Chemiosmotic hypothesis.", "Proton accumulation in lumen.", "Proton depletion in stroma."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 13 (New Ch 11)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Respiration in Plants','','expert','single','How many ATP are produced by substrate-level phosphorylation during the conversion of one molecule of glucose to two molecules of Pyruvic acid?','[{"id": "a", "text": "2 ATP"}, {"id": "b", "text": "36 ATP"}, {"id": "c", "text": "4 ATP"}, {"id": "d", "text": "8 ATP"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'During glycolysis, 4 ATP are produced by SLP (2 from BPGA to PGA, 2 from PEP to Pyruvate). Net gain is 2, but total production by SLP is 4.','["Glycolysis: Glucose to 2 Pyruvates.", "2 x (BPGA -> PGA) = 2 ATP.", "2 x (PEP -> Pyruvate) = 2 ATP.", "Total produced = 4."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 14 (New Ch 12)'),
