(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Botany','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance / Biomolecules','','expert','single','In a DNA molecule, if the percentage of Cytosine is 18%, what is the percentage of Adenine?','[{"id": "a", "text": "18%"}, {"id": "b", "text": "36%"}, {"id": "c", "text": "32%"}, {"id": "d", "text": "64%"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'C = G = 18%. Total C+G = 36%. Therefore, A+T = 100 - 36 = 64%. A = T = 64/2 = 32%.','["Chargaff''s Rule: A=T, G=C.", "G = 18%. C+G = 36%.", "A+T = 64%.", "A = 32%."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 6 (New Ch 5) / XI Ch 9'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Biomolecules','','medium','single','The ''Lock and Key'' hypothesis of enzyme action explains how a specific substrate binds to an enzyme. If an enzyme''s tertiary structure is slightly altered by a non-competitive inhibitor binding to an allosteric site, what is the most likely outcome?','[{"id": "a", "text": "The substrate will bind more tightly to the active site."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The active site changes shape, preventing substrate binding."}, {"id": "c", "text": "The inhibitor competes with the substrate for the active site."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The Vmax remains constant while Km increases."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Non-competitive inhibitors bind to the allosteric site, changing the enzyme''s shape so the substrate no longer fits the active site.','["Identify the role of allosteric sites.", "Recall that non-competitive inhibition involves conformational changes.", "Conclude that the active site is no longer functional."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 9'),
