(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Principles of Inheritance and Variation','','medium','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Human females have a higher risk of becoming carriers for Hemophilia compared to males.
Reason (R): Hemophilia is a Y-linked recessive disorder.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Hemophilia is an X-linked recessive disorder, not Y-linked. Males cannot be carriers; they are either affected or normal.','["Check the inheritance pattern of Hemophilia.", "Evaluate the reason (Y-linked vs X-linked)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 5'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Breathing and Exchange of Gases','','medium','single','The volume of air that remains in the lungs even after a forcible expiration is called:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Expiratory Reserve Volume"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tidal Volume"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Residual Volume"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vital Capacity"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Residual Volume (RV) is the air left in the lungs after maximum expiration.','["Define lung volumes.", "Identify the volume that cannot be expelled."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 17'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Body Fluids and Circulation','','hard','single','Identify the correct sequence of events during a single cardiac cycle in a healthy adult:
I. Closing of AV valves
II. Atrial systole
III. Semilunar valves open
IV. Joint diastole
V. Ventricular diastole starts
VI. Ventricular systole starts','[{"id": "a", "text": "IV -> II -> VI -> III -> I -> V"}, {"id": "b", "text": "IV -> II -> VI -> I -> III -> V"}, {"id": "c", "text": "II -> VI -> I -> IV -> III -> V"}, {"id": "d", "text": "VI -> III -> I -> V -> IV -> II"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'The cycle follows Joint diastole -> Atrial systole -> Ventricular systole (closing AV valves, opening SL valves) -> Ventricular diastole.','["Identify starting point as Joint Diastole (IV).", "Atrial systole (II) follows.", "Ventricular systole (VI) causes AV valves to close (I) before SL valves open (III).", "Ventricular diastole (V) follows."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 18'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Breathing and Exchange of Gases','','hard','single','A patient has a vital capacity (VC) of 4.5 L, Tidal Volume (TV) of 0.5 L, and Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV) of 1.2 L. If the Residual Volume (RV) is 1.1 L, what is the Inspiratory Capacity (IC) and Functional Residual Capacity (FRC)?','[{"id": "a", "text": "IC = 3.8 L, FRC = 2.8 L"}, {"id": "b", "text": "IC = 3.0 L, FRC = 1.7 L"}, {"id": "c", "text": "IC = 3.3 L, FRC = 2.3 L"}, {"id": "d", "text": "IC = 2.8 L, FRC = 2.3 L"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'IC = VC - ERV = 4.5 - 1.2 = 3.3 L; FRC = ERV + RV = 1.2 + 1.1 = 2.3 L.','["Formula: VC = IRV + TV + ERV; IC = IRV + TV.", "IC = VC - ERV = 4.5 - 1.2 = 3.3 L.", "FRC = ERV + RV = 1.2 + 1.1 = 2.3 L."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 17'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Neural Control and Coordination','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): During the conduction of a nerve impulse, the Na-K pump helps in maintaining the resting potential by transporting 3 Na+ outwards for 2 K+ inwards.
Reason (R): The axonal membrane is nearly impermeable to potassium ions (K+) and relatively permeable to sodium ions (Na+) at rest.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both A and R are false."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Assertion is true, but Reason is false because at rest, the membrane is more permeable to K+ and nearly impermeable to Na+.','["Evaluate pump stoichiometry: 3 Na+ out / 2 K+ in (True).", "Evaluate resting permeability: K+ is high, Na+ is low (Reason is false)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 21'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Excretory Products and Their Elimination','','hard','single','In the context of the counter-current mechanism in the kidney, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?','[{"id": "a", "text": "The NaCl is transported by the ascending limb of Henle''s loop which is exchanged with the descending limb of vasa recta."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The gradient in the medullary interstitium is mainly caused by NaCl and urea."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Water is highly permeable in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Urea enters the thin segment of the ascending limb of Henle''s loop."}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'NaCl is transported out of the ascending limb of the loop of Henle, not the descending limb.','["Ascending limb is impermeable to water.", "Descending limb is permeable to water.", "NaCl leaves the ascending limb."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 19'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Chemical Coordination and Integration','','hard','single','Match the following hormones with their source and function:
(a) CCK (i) Gastric glands; inhibits secretion
(b) GIP (ii) Pancreas; stimulates enzyme secretion
(c) Secretin (iii) Duodenum; stimulates water and bicarbonate
(d) Gastrin (iv) Stomach; stimulates HCl secretion','[{"id": "a", "text": "(a)-ii, (b)-i, (c)-iii, (d)-iv"}, {"id": "b", "text": "(a)-iii, (b)-ii, (c)-i, (d)-iv"}, {"id": "c", "text": "(a)-iv, (b)-iii, (c)-ii, (d)-i"}, {"id": "d", "text": "(a)-ii, (b)-iii, (c)-i, (d)-iv"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'CCK acts on the pancreas for enzymes; GIP inhibits gastric secretion; Secretin stimulates bicarbonate; Gastrin stimulates HCl.','["CCK = Cholecystokinin -> Pancreatic enzymes.", "GIP = Gastric Inhibitory Peptide -> Inhibits secretion.", "Secretin = Bicarbonate/Water.", "Gastrin = HCl."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 22'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Locomotion and Movement','','hard','single','Which of the following describes the ''Power Stroke'' in muscle contraction correctly?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Binding of ATP to the myosin head causes it to detach from actin."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Release of ADP and inorganic phosphate (Pi) makes the myosin head tilt, pulling actin towards the M-line."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hydrolysis of ATP causes the myosin head to bind to the active site on actin."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Binding of Calcium to Troponin C masks the active sites on actin."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'The release of ADP and Pi causes the myosin head to bend and pull the actin filament.','["ATP hydrolysis ''cocks'' the head.", "Binding occurs.", "Release of ADP/Pi causes the power stroke/sliding."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 20'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Principles of Inheritance and Variation','','hard','single','In a dihybrid cross involving two genes located on the same chromosome (linked), the frequency of recombination was found to be 5%. What does this indicate?','[{"id": "a", "text": "The genes are 5 map units apart and show tight linkage."}, {"id": "b", "text": "The genes show independent assortment."}, {"id": "c", "text": "The genes are 50 map units apart."}, {"id": "d", "text": "95% of the progeny are recombinants."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Recombination frequency is proportional to the distance between genes (1% = 1 cM). 5% = 5 map units.','["Recombination frequency = Distance in centiMorgans (cM).", "5% recombination = 5 cM.", "Low recombination means high linkage."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 5'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): In the follicular phase of the menstrual cycle, LH and FSH levels gradually increase.
Reason (R): The increasing level of LH and FSH stimulates follicular development and secretion of estrogens by the growing follicles.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both A and R are false."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Both statements are true and the reason explains the follicular development during the phase.','["Check FSH/LH trend in follicular phase: Rising (True).", "Check effect: Stimulates follicles/estrogen (True)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Health and Disease','','hard','single','How many of the following are examples of passive immunity?
I. Colostrum given to a newborn
II. Anti-tetanus serum (ATS) injection
III. Polio drops (OPV)
IV. Transfer of antibodies through placenta
V. Vaccination against Hepatitis B','[{"id": "a", "text": "Two"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Four"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Three"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Five"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Colostrum (IgA), ATS (pre-formed antibodies), and Placenta (IgG) are passive. Polio and Hep B vaccines are active immunity.','["Passive = Transfer of pre-formed antibodies.", "I (IgA), II (Anti-toxin), IV (IgG via placenta) are passive.", "III and V are active (body produces its own antibodies)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 8'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance','','hard','single','Which statement about the Lac Operon in E. coli is correct?','[{"id": "a", "text": "The i-gene codes for the inducer."}, {"id": "b", "text": "RNA polymerase binds to the operator to initiate transcription."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lactose binds to the repressor to inactivate it."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The y-gene codes for transacetylase."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Lactose acts as an inducer that binds to the repressor protein, preventing it from binding to the operator.','["i-gene = repressor.", "Polymerase binds to promoter.", "Lactose (Inducer) inactivates repressor.", "y-gene = Permease."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 6'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance','','hard','single','In a specific DNA molecule, 20% of the bases are Adenine. What will be the percentage of Cytosine?','[{"id": "a", "text": "20%"}, {"id": "b", "text": "30%"}, {"id": "c", "text": "60%"}, {"id": "d", "text": "80%"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Chargaff''s Rule: A=T, G=C. If A=20, then T=20. A+T=40. Remaining 60% is G+C. So C = 30%.','["A = 20%, so T = 20% (A+T = 40%).", "Total (100%) - 40% = 60% for G+C.", "C = 60/2 = 30%."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 6'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Reproductive Health','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The use of MTP (Medical Termination of Pregnancy) is always safe throughout the first and second trimesters.
Reason (R): MTP is legal in India up to 24 weeks for certain categories like rape victims.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is false but R is true."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both A and R are false."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Assertion is false; MTP is significantly riskier in the second trimester. Reason is true (amended MTP act).','["Assess MTP safety: Safest in 1st trimester only (Assertion False).", "Assess legal limit: 24 weeks for specific cases (Reason True)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 4'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Health and Disease','','hard','single','Which of the following is NOT an autoimmune disease?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Muscular dystrophy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Myasthenia gravis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rheumatoid arthritis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Muscular dystrophy is a genetic disorder, whereas Myasthenia gravis and Rheumatoid arthritis are autoimmune.','["Identify Muscular Dystrophy as genetic (dystrophin gene).", "Others involve immune attack on self-antigens."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 8 / XI Ch 20'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Breathing and Exchange of Gases','','hard','single','Calculate the Partial Pressure of Oxygen (pO2) in the Alveoli if the total atmospheric pressure is 760 mmHg and Oxygen makes up 21% of the dry air, considering water vapor pressure in alveoli? (Note: Refer to standard NCERT values).','[{"id": "a", "text": "159 mmHg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "104 mmHg"}, {"id": "c", "text": "40 mmHg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "95 mmHg"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'NCERT standard table gives pO2 in Alveoli as 104 mmHg.','["Atmospheric pO2 = 159 mmHg.", "Alveolar pO2 = 104 mmHg."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 17'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Molecular Basis of Inheritance','','hard','single','If a double-stranded DNA has 20 coils (turns of helix), what is the total number of hydrogen bonds if the number of Guanine bases is 30?','[{"id": "a", "text": "400"}, {"id": "b", "text": "500"}, {"id": "c", "text": "600"}, {"id": "d", "text": "430"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'20 coils = 200 base pairs (400 bases). G=30, so C=30 (30 G-C pairs). Remaining are A-T pairs: (200 - 30) = 170 A-T pairs. H-bonds = (30 * 3) + (170 * 2) = 90 + 340 = 430.','["1 turn = 10 bp. 20 turns = 200 bp.", "G=30, C=30. G-C pair = 3 bonds. 30 * 3 = 90.", "Remaining 170 pairs are A-T. A-T pair = 2 bonds. 170 * 2 = 340.", "Total = 90 + 340 = 430."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 6'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','hard','single','Choose the correct statement regarding the hormonal control of spermatogenesis.','[{"id": "a", "text": "GnRH acts on the posterior pituitary to release LH and FSH."}, {"id": "b", "text": "LH acts on Sertoli cells to stimulate the secretion of some factors for spermiogenesis."}, {"id": "c", "text": "FSH acts on Sertoli cells and stimulates the process of spermiogenesis."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Testosterone acts on the hypothalamus to stimulate GnRH via positive feedback."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'LH acts on Leydig cells for testosterone; FSH acts on Sertoli cells for spermiogenesis.','["GnRH -> Anterior Pituitary.", "LH -> Leydig (Androgens).", "FSH -> Sertoli (Spermiogenesis)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Reproductive Health','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Saheli is a ''once-a-week'' pill with very few side effects.
Reason (R): It is a non-steroidal preparation containing Centchroman.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both A and R are false."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Both are correct; its non-steroidal nature reduces side effects and its pharmacokinetics allow weekly dosage.','["Saheli = non-steroidal, weekly (True).", "Ingredient = Centchroman (True)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 4'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Body Fluids and Circulation','','hard','single','In a standard ECG, the T-wave represents:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Depolarization of ventricles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Repolarization of ventricles"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Depolarization of atria"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Beginning of systole"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'T-wave represents the return of ventricles to the normal state (repolarization).','["P = Atrial Depol.", "QRS = Ventricular Depol.", "T = Ventricular Repol."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 18'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','hard','single','Which of the following describes the correct path of sperm from the seminiferous tubules to the outside?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Rete testis -> Vasa efferentia -> Epididymis -> Vas deferens"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vasa efferentia -> Rete testis -> Vas deferens -> Epididymis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Epididymis -> Rete testis -> Vasa efferentia -> Vas deferens"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rete testis -> Epididymis -> Vasa efferentia -> Vas deferens"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Seminiferous tubules -> Rete testis -> Vasa efferentia -> Epididymis -> Vas deferens -> Ejaculatory duct -> Urethra.','["Start at Rete testis.", "Next is Vasa efferentia (out of testis).", "Then storage in Epididymis.", "Then Vas deferens."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Animal Kingdom','','hard','single','Identify the mismatch in the following:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Spongilla - Canal system"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pennatula - Cnidocytes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pleurobrachia - Organ level of organization"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ancylostoma - Pseudocoelomate"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Ctenophores exhibit tissue level of organization, not organ level.','["Pleurobrachia (Ctenophora) = Tissue level.", "Others are correctly matched."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 4'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Neural Control and Coordination','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The inner ear contains the organ of Corti which acts as an auditory receptor.
Reason (R): The organ of Corti is located on the Reissner''s membrane.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both A and R are false."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Assertion is true, but the organ of Corti is located on the Basilar membrane, not Reissner''s membrane.','["Locate Organ of Corti: Inner ear (True).", "Locate base: Basilar membrane (Reason False)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 21'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Organisms and Populations / Breathing and Exchange of Gases','','hard','single','A person who has migrated from the plains to Rohtang Pass (~4000m) for six months will:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Have a lower RBC count than plains dwellers."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Have a higher RBC count and their hemoglobin has a lower binding affinity for O2."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Suffer from altitude sickness permanently."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Show no change in breathing rate."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Altitude sickness is compensated by increasing RBC count, decreasing hemoglobin binding affinity, and increasing breathing rate.','["Low pO2 at altitude triggers adaptation.", "Adaptation = more RBCs + lower Hb affinity (to release O2 to tissues)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 13 / XI Ch 17'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Excretory Products and Their Elimination','','hard','single','Which of the following sets of animals are all Uricotelic?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Bony fish, Aquatic amphibians, Insects"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mammals, Terrestrial amphibians, Sharks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Birds, Reptiles, Bony fish"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reptiles, Birds, Insects"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Reptiles, birds, land snails, and insects are uricotelic. Birds and Reptiles fit this.','["Ammonotelic = Aquatic.", "Ureotelic = Mammals/Terrestrial Amphibians.", "Uricotelic = Birds/Reptiles/Insects."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 19'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Biotechnology and its Applications','','hard','single','During the processing of pro-insulin into mature insulin in humans:','[{"id": "a", "text": "C-peptide is added to the pro-insulin."}, {"id": "b", "text": "C-peptide is removed from the pro-insulin."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A and B chains are joined by hydrogen bonds."}, {"id": "d", "text": "The process occurs in the Golgi apparatus of the E. coli used for production."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'C-peptide is removed during maturation; mature insulin has A and B chains linked by disulfide bonds.','["Pro-insulin = A + B + C.", "Mature insulin = A + B.", "C-peptide is removed."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 12'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Health and Disease','','hard','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Morphine is a very effective sedative and painkiller.
Reason (R): It is extracted from the latex of the Papaver somniferum plant.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both A and R are false."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Both statements are true, but the source of the drug (R) does not explain why it acts as a sedative/painkiller (A).','["Morphine use: Sedative/Painkiller (True).", "Morphine source: Poppy latex (True).", "Source does not explain pharmacology."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 8'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Biomolecules','','hard','single','The ''Lock and Key'' hypothesis of enzyme action explains:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Enzyme specificity"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Enzyme denaturation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Non-competitive inhibition"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Activation energy increase"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'It explains enzyme specificity where the substrate fits into a specific active site.','["Active site shape is specific to substrate.", "Specificity is the core of the hypothesis."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 9'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Principles of Inheritance and Variation','','hard','single','How many barr bodies will be present in a person with Klinefelter syndrome?','[{"id": "a", "text": "0"}, {"id": "b", "text": "1"}, {"id": "c", "text": "2"}, {"id": "d", "text": "3"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Klinefelter is XXY. Barr bodies = No. of X chromosomes - 1. So, 2 - 1 = 1.','["Identify genotype: XXY.", "Formula: X - 1.", "2 - 1 = 1."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 5'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Cell Cycle and Cell Division','','hard','single','Which stage of Prophase I is characterized by the appearance of recombination nodules?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Zygotene"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Diplotene"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pachytene"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Diakinesis"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Pachytene is where crossing over occurs and recombination nodules appear.','["Leptotene (Condensation).", "Zygotene (Synapsis).", "Pachytene (Crossing over/Nodules)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 10'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Evolution','','hard','single','Which of the following is an example of an Analogous structure?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Forelimbs of Whale and Bat"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Thorns of Bougainvillea and Tendrils of Cucurbita"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Heart of Bird and Mammal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sweet potato and Potato"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Sweet potato (root) and Potato (stem) have different origins but similar storage functions. Others are homologous.','["Analogous = Different origin, same function.", "Sweet potato (Root), Potato (Stem). Both store food."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 7'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','hard','single','In the human female, the second meiotic division is completed:','[{"id": "a", "text": "At the time of ovulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "During the embryonic development"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediately after birth"}, {"id": "d", "text": "At the time of fusion of a sperm with an ovum"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Meiosis II is completed only when the sperm enters the ovum (fertilization).','["Oogenesis arrests at Prophase I before birth.", "Arrests at Metaphase II during ovulation.", "Completes at fertilization."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Locomotion and Movement','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): During muscle contraction, the length of the A-band remains constant while the I-band shortens.
Reason (R): The myosin filaments do not change their length, but the actin filaments slide over them, reducing the distance between Z-lines.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The sliding filament theory states that during contraction, actin filaments slide towards the M-line, shortening the H-zone and I-band while A-band length is conserved.','["Identify the structural components of a sarcomere.", "Recall the sliding filament mechanism.", "Analyze the behavior of A and I bands during contraction."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 20 (Locomotion and Movement)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Body Fluids and Circulation','','expert','single','In the human heart, the volume of blood pumped by the left ventricle into the systemic circulation per minute is approximately 5 litres. If the end-diastolic volume is 120 ml and the end-systolic volume is 50 ml, what is the heart rate?','[{"id": "a", "text": "60 bpm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "80 bpm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "72 bpm"}, {"id": "d", "text": "90 bpm"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Cardiac Output = Stroke Volume x Heart Rate. Stroke Volume = EDV - ESV = 70 ml. 5000 / 70 ≈ 71.4, which is approximately 72 bpm.','["Calculate Stroke Volume: 120 - 50 = 70 ml.", "Use Cardiac Output formula: 5000 ml = 70 ml x HR.", "Solve for HR: 5000 / 70 = 71.42."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 18 (Body Fluids and Circulation)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Excretory Products and Their Elimination','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): In the kidney, the descending limb of the loop of Henle is impermeable to electrolytes but permeable to water.
Reason (R): This permeability profile helps in concentrating the filtrate as it moves towards the medulla.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The descending limb allows water reabsorption into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, concentrating the tubular fluid.','["Recall the permeability of the descending limb.", "Determine the effect of this permeability on filtrate concentration."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 19 (Excretory Products and Their Elimination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Chemical Coordination and Integration','','expert','single','Which of the following hormones is a polypeptide but acts through a secondary messenger like cAMP?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Cortisol"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Epinephrine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Testosterone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Glucagon"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Glucagon is a peptide hormone that binds to membrane receptors and uses cAMP, unlike steroids or iodothyronines.','["Categorize the hormones by chemical nature.", "Identify the mechanism of action (membrane vs. intracellular).", "Glucagon is a polypeptide using a second messenger."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 22 (Chemical Coordination and Integration)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Breathing and Exchange of Gases','','expert','single','A person''s vital capacity is 4500 ml, their expiratory reserve volume is 1100 ml, and their tidal volume is 500 ml. What is their inspiratory capacity?','[{"id": "a", "text": "3000 ml"}, {"id": "b", "text": "3400 ml"}, {"id": "c", "text": "2900 ml"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1600 ml"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Vital Capacity (VC) = IRV + TV + ERV. Inspiratory Capacity (IC) = IRV + TV. VC = IC + ERV -> IC = 4500 - 1100 = 3400 ml.','["State the formula for Vital Capacity: VC = TV + IRV + ERV.", "State the formula for Inspiratory Capacity: IC = TV + IRV.", "Substitute values: 4500 = IC + 1100.", "Solve for IC: 3400 ml."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 17 (Breathing and Exchange of Gases)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Neural Control and Coordination','','expert','single','Identify the correct sequence of cells in the human retina from inside (towards vitreous humor) to outside:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Photoreceptors -> Bipolar cells -> Ganglion cells"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bipolar cells -> Ganglion cells -> Photoreceptors"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ganglion cells -> Bipolar cells -> Photoreceptors"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Photoreceptors -> Ganglion cells -> Bipolar cells"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Light passes through the ganglion cells first, then bipolar cells, and finally reaches the photoreceptors (rods and cones).','["Recall the layered structure of the retina.", "Note the direction of light entry.", "Sequence: Ganglion -> Bipolar -> Photoreceptors."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 21 (Neural Control and Coordination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Excretory Products and Their Elimination','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) plays a complex regulatory role in the functioning of the kidneys.
Reason (R): A fall in glomerular blood pressure can activate the JG cells to release renin which converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'JGA regulates GFR via the Renin-Angiotensin mechanism, where renin release is triggered by low pressure/volume.','["Define JGA and its location.", "Recall the triggers for Renin release.", "Connect Renin to the conversion of angiotensinogen."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 19 (Excretory Products and Their Elimination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','expert','single','In human females, the first polar body is formed during:','[{"id": "a", "text": "The completion of Meiosis II after fertilization"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The completion of Meiosis I during oogenesis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The transition from primary oocyte to oogonia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The formation of the ootid"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'The first polar body is a result of the completion of Meiosis I within the Graafian follicle just before ovulation.','["Differentiate between Meiosis I and II in oogenesis.", "Recall that Meiosis I results in a secondary oocyte and the first polar body.", "Recall that Meiosis II results in an ovum and the second polar body."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3 (Human Reproduction)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Structural Organisation in Animals','','expert','single','Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of the connective tissue ''Blood''?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Originates from the mesoderm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Lack of structural fibres in the fluid matrix normally"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Presence of phagocytic cells like monocytes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Presence of collagen and elastin in the plasma"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Blood is a fluid connective tissue that contains fibers only during the clotting process (fibrin), not in its normal fluid state.','["Identify blood as a specialized connective tissue.", "Recall the components of plasma and formed elements.", "Note the exception regarding fibers in blood vs other connective tissues."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 7 (Structural Organisation in Animals)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Excretory Products and Their Elimination','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Presence of a high concentration of glucose and ketone bodies in urine is indicative of Diabetes Mellitus.
Reason (R): In Diabetes Mellitus, the body''s inability to utilize glucose leads to the breakdown of fats, producing ketone bodies.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Glycosuria and Ketonuria are classic clinical indicators of untreated Diabetes Mellitus due to metabolic shifts.','["Link high glucose to insulin deficiency/resistance.", "Explain the metabolic origin of ketone bodies.", "Confirm both terms (Glycosuria/Ketonuria) relate to Diabetes."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 19 (Excretory Products and Their Elimination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Chemical Coordination and Integration','','expert','single','A patient has an abnormally high level of Calcium in their blood. Which hormone is likely to be under-secreted?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aldosterone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Thyrocalcitonin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Thyroxine"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Thyrocalcitonin (TCT) lowers blood calcium levels, while Parathyroid hormone (PTH) increases it. Hypercalcemia suggests low TCT or high PTH.','["Identify the hormones regulating calcium.", "Determine the effect of TCT (calcitonin).", "Determine the effect of PTH.", "Match under-secretion of a ''lowering'' hormone to high blood levels."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 22 (Chemical Coordination and Integration)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Breathing and Exchange of Gases','','expert','single','In the context of O2 transport, what would cause a ''Right Shift'' in the Oxygen-Hemoglobin dissociation curve?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Low pCO2 and high pH"}, {"id": "b", "text": "High pCO2 and low pH"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Low temperature and high pO2"}, {"id": "d", "text": "High pO2 and low H+ concentration"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'A right shift (lower affinity) is caused by high pCO2, low pH (high H+), and high temperature, typical of active tissues.','["Define Right Shift as decreased affinity.", "Recall the factors favoring dissociation in tissues.", "Link pCO2 and H+ to the Bohr effect."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 17 (Breathing and Exchange of Gases)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Reproductive Health','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): Intra-cytoplasmic Sperm Injection (ICSI) is a procedure where a sperm is directly injected into the ovum.
Reason (R): It is used in cases where the male partner has very low sperm count or inability to inseminate.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Both A and R are true; R explains why the procedure is chosen, but A describes the procedure itself. (Usually considered correct explanation in NEET context).','["Define ICSI according to NCERT.", "Identify clinical indications for ICSI.", "Check if R explains why A is performed."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 4 (Reproductive Health)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Excretory Products and Their Elimination','','expert','single','The net filtration pressure (NFP) in a nephron is calculated based on: Glomerular Blood Hydrostatic Pressure (GBHP) = 60 mmHg, Capsular Hydrostatic Pressure (CHP) = 15 mmHg, and Blood Colloidal Osmotic Pressure (BCOP) = 30 mmHg. What is the NFP?','[{"id": "a", "text": "15 mmHg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "25 mmHg"}, {"id": "c", "text": "45 mmHg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "105 mmHg"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'NFP = GBHP - (CHP + BCOP) = 60 - (15 + 30) = 15 mmHg.','["List the pressures favoring filtration (GBHP).", "List the pressures opposing filtration (CHP, BCOP).", "Apply the formula: NFP = Favors - Opposes."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 19 (Excretory Products and Their Elimination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Neural Control and Coordination','','expert','single','Which part of the brain is responsible for controlling body temperature, urge for eating and drinking?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Thalamus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cerebellum"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypothalamus"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Medulla oblongata"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'The Hypothalamus contains centers for thermoregulation and controls appetite (hunger and satiety) and thirst.','["Recall functions of the Forebrain.", "Identify the role of the Hypothalamus in homeostasis.", "Match hunger/thirst/temp to the correct region."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 21 (Neural Control and Coordination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Body Fluids and Circulation','','expert','single','In a person with blood group AB, which antibodies are present in the plasma?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Anti-A antibodies"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Anti-B antibodies"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Both Anti-A and Anti-B antibodies"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neither Anti-A nor Anti-B antibodies"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Blood group AB individuals have both A and B antigens on their RBCs and thus have no anti-A or anti-B antibodies in their plasma.','["Recall the ABO blood grouping system.", "Antigens on RBC determine the group.", "Plasma contains antibodies opposite to the antigens present."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 18 (Body Fluids and Circulation)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Chemical Coordination and Integration','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The posterior pituitary gland does not synthesize any hormones.
Reason (R): It only stores and releases hormones (Oxytocin and Vasopressin) actually synthesized by the hypothalamus.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is under direct neural regulation and acts as a storage-release site.','["Identify the two lobes of the pituitary.", "Clarify the synthesis site of ADH and Oxytocin.", "Confirm the role of the neurohypophysis."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 22 (Chemical Coordination and Integration)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Digestion and Absorption','','expert','single','During the ''Felling of Hunger'', which hormone is most likely to be elevated in the bloodstream?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Pepsin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Chymotrypsin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hydrochloric acid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Enterokinase"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'While NCERT focuses on gastrin/secretin, Ghrelin (though less emphasized) is the ''hunger hormone''. Note: If strictly NCERT, questions focus on Gastrin/Secretin. Let''s switch to a strict NCERT digestive enzyme question for JEE-level rigor. 
Revised Body: Which of the following is an ACTIVATOR for the enzyme trypsinogen?','["Identify proenzymes in pancreatic juice.", "Recall the role of the intestinal mucosa.", "Identify Enterokinase as the catalyst for trypsinogen activation."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 16 (Digestion and Absorption)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','expert','single','What is the function of the ''Sertoli cells'' in the human male reproductive system?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Nutrition to germ cells"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Secretion of testosterone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Formation of the blood-testis barrier only"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Secretion of LH and FSH"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Sertoli cells, found in seminiferous tubules, provide nourishment to the developing sperms (spermatogonia/spermatids).','["Locate Sertoli cells within the testis.", "Compare functions of Leydig cells vs Sertoli cells.", "Identify ''nurse cell'' function."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3 (Human Reproduction)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Body Fluids and Circulation','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The SA node is called the pacemaker of the heart.
Reason (R): It can generate the maximum number of action potentials (70-75/min) and is responsible for initiating the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The SA node''s intrinsic rate is the highest, allowing it to drive the rest of the heart''s conduction system.','["Identify the components of the Nodal tissue.", "Compare rates of SA node vs AV node.", "Define the role of a pacemaker."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 18 (Body Fluids and Circulation)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Digestion and Absorption','','expert','single','Which of the following molecules is NOT primarily absorbed via active transport in the small intestine?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Glucose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fructose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Amino acids"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sodium ions"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Fructose is absorbed via facilitated diffusion using a carrier protein, unlike glucose and amino acids which often use active transport.','["Recall mechanisms of absorption: simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, active transport.", "Identify specific transport for Glucose (Active/Facilitated).", "Identify specific transport for Fructose (Facilitated)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 16 (Digestion and Absorption)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Body Fluids and Circulation','','expert','single','A person has a genetic defect where the ''P-wave'' of their ECG is missing. Which event is likely not occurring correctly?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Ventricular depolarization"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ventricular repolarization"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Atrial depolarization"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Atrial repolarization"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'The P-wave represents the electrical excitation (depolarization) of the atria, leading to atrial contraction.','["Map ECG waves to cardiac events.", "P = Atrial Depolarization. QRS = Ventricular Depolarization. T = Ventricular Repolarization."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 18 (Body Fluids and Circulation)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Neural Control and Coordination','','expert','single','In the human ear, the ''Organ of Corti'' is located on which of the following membranes?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Basilar membrane"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reissner''s membrane"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tectorial membrane"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tympanic membrane"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The Organ of Corti, containing hair cells, sits on the basilar membrane within the cochlea.','["Recall the anatomy of the Cochlea.", "Identify the three scales (vestibuli, media, tympani).", "Identify the membrane supporting the sensory organ."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 21 (Neural Control and Coordination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): During pregnancy, high levels of estrogen and progesterone inhibit the release of FSH and LH.
Reason (R): High levels of these steroids provide negative feedback to the pituitary to prevent further ovulation during gestation.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Steroid levels maintained by the corpus luteum and later the placenta prevent new follicles from developing.','["Identify hormone levels during pregnancy.", "Understand negative feedback on the GnRH-FSH/LH axis.", "Determine the physiological outcome (no ovulation)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3 (Human Reproduction)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Digestion and Absorption / Chemical Coordination','','expert','single','Which hormone acts on the exocrine part of the pancreas and stimulates the secretion of water and bicarbonate ions?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Gastrin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Secretin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cholecystokinin (CCK)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Gastric Inhibitory Peptide (GIP)"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Secretin acts on the exocrine pancreas for bicarbonates, whereas CCK acts on the pancreas for enzymes and the gallbladder for bile.','["Differentiate between Secretin and CCK.", "Identify the specific trigger for water/bicarbonate."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 22 (Chemical Coordination and Integration)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Structural Organisation in Animals','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): The squamous epithelium is found in the walls of blood vessels and air sacs of lungs.
Reason (R): Being thin, it is involved in functions like forming a diffusion boundary.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Simple squamous epithelium consists of a single thin layer of flattened cells, ideal for rapid diffusion.','["Relate cell shape to function.", "Identify locations where diffusion is critical.", "Verify NCERT examples for squamous tissue."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 7 (Structural Organisation in Animals)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Breathing and Exchange of Gases','','expert','single','What is the partial pressure of Oxygen (pO2) in the systemic arteries (oxygenated blood)?','[{"id": "a", "text": "40 mm Hg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "45 mm Hg"}, {"id": "c", "text": "95 mm Hg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "104 mm Hg"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'According to the NCERT table, pO2 in oxygenated blood is 95 mm Hg, while pCO2 is 40 mm Hg.','["Recall the table of partial pressures.", "Differentiate between alveolar air (104), oxygenated blood (95), and deoxygenated blood (40)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 17 (Breathing and Exchange of Gases)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Locomotion and Movement','','expert','single','Which of the following is a symptom of ''Gout''?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Inflammation of joints due to low calcium"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Degeneration of skeletal muscle due to genetic disorder"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Autoimmune disorder affecting neuromuscular junction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inflammation of joints due to accumulation of uric acid crystals"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Gout is a specific type of arthritis caused by the accumulation of uric acid crystals in joints.','["Differentiate between Myasthenia gravis, Muscular dystrophy, Tetany, and Gout.", "Identify the biochemical cause of Gout."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 20 (Locomotion and Movement)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Neural Control and Coordination','','expert','assertion_reason','Assertion (A): In a myelinated nerve fibre, the nerve impulse jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next.
Reason (R): The myelin sheath is continuous and prevents the leakage of ions, speeding up the conduction.','[{"id": "a", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."}, {"id": "c", "text": "A is true but R is false."}, {"id": "d", "text": "A is false but R is true."}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'The myelin sheath is NOT continuous; it is interrupted at nodes of Ranvier, which is where saltatory conduction occurs.','["Define saltatory conduction.", "Identify the structure of a myelinated axon.", "Check the ''continuous'' claim for the myelin sheath."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 21 (Neural Control and Coordination)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Breathing and Exchange of Gases (Integration)','','expert','single','If a man has a normal tidal volume of 500 ml and a breathing rate of 12 times/minute, but his anatomical dead space is 150 ml, what is his alveolar ventilation rate?','[{"id": "a", "text": "6000 ml/min"}, {"id": "b", "text": "4200 ml/min"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1800 ml/min"}, {"id": "d", "text": "5850 ml/min"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Alveolar Ventilation = (Tidal Volume - Dead Space) x Breathing Rate = (500 - 150) x 12 = 350 x 12 = 4200 ml/min.','["Define Minute Ventilation: TV x RR.", "Define Alveolar Ventilation: (TV - Dead Space) x RR.", "Calculate: 350 x 12."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 17 (Breathing and Exchange of Gases)'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Chemical Coordination and Integration','','easy','single','Which of the following hormones is a steroid hormone?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Glucagon"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cortisol"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insulin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Thyroxine"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'Cortisol is a glucocorticoid produced by the adrenal cortex and is steroid in nature.','["Identify chemical nature of hormones listed.", "Glucagon and Insulin are peptides.", "Thyroxine is iodinated amino acid derivative.", "Cortisol is a steroid."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 22'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Breathing and Exchange of Gases','','easy','single','During inspiration, the diaphragm:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Contracts and flattens"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Relaxes and becomes dome-shaped"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Contracts and becomes dome-shaped"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Relaxes and flattens"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'Contraction of the diaphragm increases the volume of the thoracic chamber in the antero-posterior axis.','["Identify the mechanism of inspiration.", "Diaphragm contracts to increase thoracic volume.", "Contracted diaphragm moves downwards (flattens)."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 17'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Body Fluids and Circulation','','easy','single','What is the life span of an Erythrocyte (RBC) in a healthy adult human?','[{"id": "a", "text": "100 days"}, {"id": "b", "text": "60 days"}, {"id": "c", "text": "150 days"}, {"id": "d", "text": "120 days"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'RBCs have an average life span of 120 days, after which they are destroyed in the spleen.','["Recall RBC physiology.", "State the average lifespan."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 18'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Body Fluids and Circulation','','easy','single','The standard ECG, the ''T-wave'' represents:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Atrial depolarisation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ventricular depolarisation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ventricular repolarisation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Atrial repolarisation"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'The T-wave represents the return of the ventricles from excited to normal state (repolarisation).','["Identify ECG components: P, QRS, T.", "T-wave corresponds to the end of systole and ventricular recovery."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 18'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Excretory Products and their Elimination','','easy','single','Which part of the renal tubule is impermeable to water?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Proximal Convoluted Tubule"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ascending limb of Loop of Henle"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Descending limb of Loop of Henle"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Distal Convoluted Tubule"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'The ascending limb of Henle''s loop is impermeable to water but allows transport of electrolytes.','["Review permeability of nephron segments.", "Descending limb is permeable to water.", "Ascending limb is impermeable to water."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 19'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Locomotion and Movement','','easy','single','The H-zone in the skeletal muscle fibre is due to:','[{"id": "a", "text": "The central gap between actin filaments extending through myosin filaments"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Extension of myosin filaments in the central portion of the A-band"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The absence of myofibrils in the central portion of A-band"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The central gap between myosin filaments"}]'::jsonb,'"a"'::jsonb,'The H-zone is the central part of the thick filament (A-band) not overlapped by thin filaments.','["Recall Sarcomere structure.", "A-band contains both thick and thin filaments.", "H-zone is the ''thick-only'' central region."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 20'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Locomotion and Movement','','easy','single','Which of the following joints is an example of a Ball and Socket joint?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Knee joint"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Atlas and Axis joint"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Carpal and Metacarpal of thumb"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Humerus and Pectoral girdle"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'The joint between the humerus and pectoral girdle (shoulder joint) is a ball and socket joint.','["Identify joint types.", "Knee is hinge, Atlas/Axis is pivot.", "Humerus/Pectoral girdle is ball and socket."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 20'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Neural Control and Coordination','','easy','single','The part of the human brain that controls body temperature and the urge for eating is the:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Thalamus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypothalamus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cerebellum"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Medulla oblongata"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'The hypothalamus contains centers which control body temperature, urge for eating and drinking.','["Locate homeostatic control centers in the brain.", "Hypothalamus is the master regulator for temperature and hunger."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 21'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Neural Control and Coordination','','easy','single','In the human eye, the ''Blind Spot'' is the region where:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Only cones are present"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only rods are present"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Photoreceptor cells are absent"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Macula lutea is located"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'The optic nerves leave the eye and retinal blood vessels enter it at the blind spot, where photoreceptor cells are absent.','["Define blind spot anatomy.", "Identify presence/absence of rods and cones."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XI Ch 21'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','easy','single','Which layer of the uterus undergoes cyclical changes during the menstrual cycle?','[{"id": "a", "text": "Perimetrium"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Endometrium"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Myometrium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mesometrium"}]'::jsonb,'"b"'::jsonb,'The endometrium is the inner glandular layer that undergoes periodic changes during the menstrual cycle.','["Identify uterine wall layers.", "Myometrium handles contractions.", "Endometrium sheds during menstruation."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Human Reproduction','','easy','single','The process of release of a mature ovum from the Graafian follicle is called:','[{"id": "a", "text": "Fertilisation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Implantation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Spermiogenesis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ovulation"}]'::jsonb,'"d"'::jsonb,'Ovulation is the release of the secondary oocyte from the ovary after the rupture of the Graafian follicle.','["Define reproductive terminology.", "Identify the event of egg release."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 3'),
(ARRAY['kcet','neet-ug','scholarship'],'Zoology','Class 11-12',2026,'Reproductive Health','','easy','single','The ''Saheli'' contraceptive pill was developed by scientists at:','[{"id": "a", "text": "AIIMS, Delhi"}, {"id": "b", "text": "IISC, Bangalore"}, {"id": "c", "text": "CDRI, Lucknow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "ICMR, New Delhi"}]'::jsonb,'"c"'::jsonb,'Saheli, the once-a-week non-steroidal oral pill, was developed by CDRI in Lucknow.','["Recall facts about Saheli.", "Identify the research institute."]'::jsonb,'Original NCERT-aligned question — Study at Bangalore (AI-assisted draft, pending teacher review)',false,'verified','NCERT Biology XII Ch 4'),
